Exercise A: Explaining the SDLC
Prompt 1: Zero-Shot
Prompt
What is the Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC)? Explain its main phases and why they are important.
AI Response
The Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) is a process used by software development teams to plan, create, test, deploy, and maintain software.
The maiare:
Planning – The team defines the project goals, scope, resources, and schedule.
Requirements Analysis – The team identifies what users and the business need from the software.
Design – The team plans the structure, interface, database, and technical architecture.
Development – Developers write the code and build the software.
Testing – The software is tested to find and fix errors.
Deployment – The completed software is released to users.
Maintenance – The software is updated, improved, and fixed after release.
The SDLC helps teams organize their work, reduce errors, manage resources, and develop software that meets user needs.
Summary
The response explains SDLC as a process for developing and maintaining software. It identifies seven main phases, from planning to maintenance. Each phase has a specific purpose in the software development process. The response is simple and gives a good basic understanding of SDLC.

Prompt 2: Role Prompting
Prompt
You are an experienced software engineering teacher. Explain the Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) to beginner students who have little knowledge of software development. Use simple language and organize your answer in a numbered list showing the seven main phases, what happens in each phase, and why each phase is important.
AI Response
The Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) is a step-by-step process used by software teams to develop, test, release, and maintain software.
1. Planning
What happens: The team defines the project goals, scope, resources, and timeline.
Why it is important: It gives the team direction and helps manage time and resources.
2. Requirements Analysis
What happens: The team identifies what users and the business need from the software.
Why it is important: It ensures that developers understand what the software should do.
3. Design
What happens: The team plans how the software will look and work, including the interface, database, and system structure.
Why it is important: It gives developers a clear plan for building the software.
4. Development
What happens: Developers write the code and create the software.
Why it is important: This is where the planned design becomes a working application.
5. Testing
What happens: The software is checked for bugs, errors, security issues, and other problems.
Why it is important: Testing improves quality and helps ensure the software works correctly.
6. Deployment
What happens: The completed software is released to users.
Why it is important: Users can begin using the software in a real environment.
7. Maintenance
What happens: Developers fix problems, provide updates, and improve the software.
Why it is important: Maintenance keeps the software useful, secure, and reliable.
Summary
The second response is more detailed and easier for beginners to understand. The teacher role encouraged the AI to explain the topic in a simple teaching style. The numbered format makes the seven phases easy to follow. It also explains both what happens and why each phase is important.
Compare
The Role Prompting response was more useful because it was clearer and better organized for beginners. I changed the prompt by giving the AI a teacher role, specifying the audience, and requesting a numbered format. The role helped the AI use a teaching style, while the audience requirement encouraged it to use simple language. The format requirement made the response easier to read and understand.

Exercise B: Software Development Models
Prompt
You are a software development consultant helping a company choose the best software development model. For each situation below, recommend the most appropriate development model. Explain why it is suitable and briefly mention one limitation of the model.
A project with stable, clearly defined requirements.
A startup where requirements change frequently based on user feedback.
A large project with significant technical and security risks.
A project where testing and verification are extremely important.
Present your answer in a clear table with the situation, recommended model, reason, and limitation.
AI Response
Situation
Recommended Model
Why It Is Appropriate
Limitation
Stable, clearly defined requirements
Waterfall
Waterfall is suitable because the requirements are clear and unlikely to change. It follows a structured sequence of stages.
It is difficult to make major changes after development begins.
Startup with frequently changing requirements
Agile
Agile uses short development cycles and regular customer feedback, making it suitable for changing requirements.
The final cost and timeline can be difficult to predict.
Large project with technical and security risks
Spiral
Spiral focuses on identifying and managing risks throughout development, making it suitable for high-risk projects.
It can be complex and expensive to manage.
Testing and verification are extremely important
V-Model
The V-Model emphasizes testing and verification alongside development activities.
It is less flexible when requirements change.

Reflection
I learned that the best development model depends on the characteristics of the project. Waterfall is suitable when requirements are stable, while Agile is better when requirements change frequently. Spiral is useful for projects with high technical and security risks because it focuses on risk management. The V-Model is appropriate when testing and verification are very important.

Exercise C: Requirements Extraction
Prompt
You are a software requirements analyst. Analyze the following client brief for a local bookstore:
Track books, quantities, and prices.
Manage supplier payments.
Alert staff when stock is low.
Identify best-selling books.
Allow customers to check availability online.
Produce clear records for a part-time assistant.
The owner has a limited budget.
Organize the requirements into:
Functional requirements.
Non-functional requirements.
Must Have requirements.
Should Have requirements.
Nice to Have requirements.
Questions or ambiguities that need clarification.
Use simple language and consider the limited budget when prioritizing the requirements.
AI Response
1. Functional Requirements
The system should store book information.
The system should record book quantities and prices.
The system should manage supplier payment records.
The system should alert staff when stock is low.
The system should identify best-selling books.
Customers should be able to check book availability online.
The system should produce clear records for the part-time assistant.
Staff should be able to update book and stock information.
2. Non-Functional Requirements
Usability: The system should be simple and easy to use.
Performance: Book information should load quickly.
Security: Only authorized staff should access sensitive payment information.
Reliability: The system should accurately store and retrieve records.
Affordability: The system should fit within the bookstore's limited budget.
Availability: The online availability feature should be accessible to customers when the system is operating.
3. Must Have Requirements
Track books, quantities, and prices.
Manage supplier payments.
Provide low-stock alerts.
Identify best-selling books.
Provide clear records for the assistant.
Allow customers to check book availability online.
4. Should Have Requirements
Basic inventory and sales reports.
Staff user accounts.
Search and filtering for books.
A simple dashboard showing stock levels.
5. Nice to Have Requirements
Mobile application.
Customer reviews and ratings.
Automated supplier notifications.
Advanced sales analytics.
Personalized book recommendations.
6. Questions or Ambiguities
How many books will the system manage?
How often should stock be updated?
What quantity should trigger a low-stock alert?
Should customers only check availability or also reserve books?
What supplier payment information should be stored?
Who should have access to payment information?
Does the bookstore already have a website?
What is the exact available budget?
Should the system work on mobile phones?
What records or reports does the assistant need?
Evaluation
Two Things the AI Did Well
It correctly separated functional requirements from non-functional requirements.
It considered the limited budget when prioritizing the requirements.
One Thing the AI Missed or Categorized Incorrectly
The AI added some extra features that were not mentioned in the client brief, such as personalized recommendations and customer reviews. These could increase the cost and may not be necessary for the bookstore.
One Improvement to the Prompt
I would improve the prompt by asking the AI to clearly separate requirements directly stated by the client from additional suggestions. This would prevent unnecessary features from being treated as requirements.

Exercise D: Prompt Iteration Challenge
Version 1
Prompt
Create a project plan for a mobile application that allows university students to book campus study rooms.
AI Response
A project plan could include:
Planning the project.
Gathering requirements.
Designing the application.
Developing the application.
Testing the application.
Launching the application.
Maintaining the application.
What Is Missing?
The response is too general. It does not include time estimates, deliverables, project risks, risk mitigation, team roles, or a budget. It also does not provide enough information about how the booking application should work.

Version 2
Prompt
Create a project plan for a mobile application that allows university students to book campus study rooms. The application should allow students to view available rooms, select a date and time, make a booking, and cancel a booking. Include the main development phases, important tasks, and expected outcomes.
What I Changed
I added more information about the application and explained the main features students need.
Why
Adding context helps the AI understand the purpose of the application and produce a more relevant project plan.

Version 3
Prompt
You are a software project manager. Create a project plan for a mobile application that allows university students to book campus study rooms. Students should be able to view available rooms, check room capacity, select a date and time, make or cancel bookings, and receive booking confirmations. The system should prevent double bookings. Organize the project using the seven SDLC phases and include major activities, deliverables, and possible risks.
What I Changed
I added a role, more application requirements, all seven SDLC phases, deliverables, and project risks.
Why
The role helps the AI respond like a project manager. The additional requirements give the AI enough context to create a realistic plan. Asking for SDLC phases, deliverables, and risks makes the answer more complete.

